Acute Pulmonary Oedema (Cardiogenic)
Acute Urgent Care
Profound orthopnea, Pink frothy sputum, Bilateral fine crackles, S3 gallop

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AUC-012
Reason for Visit: Severe, sudden-onset shortness of breath with coughing up pink, frothy fluid.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated acute respiratory distress waking the patient from sleep, accompanied by gasping for air and coughing up blood-tinged sputum. Urgent home assessment and immediate medical therapy were essential to reverse the acute heart failure and prevent respiratory arrest, as the patient's condition was incompatible with waiting for regular consulting hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I woke up gasping, and I feel like I am drowning from the inside; I can't breathe unless I sit straight up!
History of Present Illness: The patient experienced paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea, waking abruptly 90 minutes ago gasping for air. The severe acute respiratory distress is characterized by profound orthopnea, requiring the patient to sit bolt upright on the edge of the bed to breathe. A severe, continuous cough has developed, producing frothy, pink, blood-tinged sputum over the last 30 minutes. The patient reports extreme chest tightness and a feeling of impending doom.
Functional Status: Sitting bolt upright, severely short of breath; unable to walk.
Social/Safety Concerns: Patient is exhausted from the work of breathing; high risk of imminent respiratory failure.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 185/105 | HR: 115 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 32 | SpO2: 88% | Wt: 88 Kg
General: Patient is in extreme respiratory distress, using accessory muscles, and has cool, clammy, diaphoretic skin reflecting poor peripheral perfusion.
Physical Exam: Auscultation reveals widespread, bilateral fine inspiratory crackles starting at the lung bases and extending to the mid-zones. Cardiovascular examination demonstrates elevated jugular venous pressure indicating right-sided fluid overload, and auscultation reveals a clear third heart sound (S3) gallop, confirming left ventricular failure. Bilateral pitting edema (2+) is present in the lower extremities to the mid-calf. Expected investigations would show markedly elevated serum BNP levels and a chest radiograph demonstrating cardiomegaly, prominent upper lobe vessels, and Kerley B lines.
Environmental Observations: CPAP machine for sleep apnea is present but was not worn tonight.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient missed regular Furosemide dose yesterday; currently taking Ramipril.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Pulmonary Oedema (Cardiogenic) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Asthma/COPD Exacerbation - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of an S3 gallop, elevated JVP, pink frothy sputum, and fine inspiratory crackles rather than widespread expiratory polyphonic wheezing.

Pulmonary Embolism - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of profound fluid overload signs (pitting edema, JVP) and the production of frothy sputum, which point to a pump failure rather than a primary vascular occlusion.

Community-Acquired Pneumonia - Ruled out/unlikely due to the rapid, acute onset waking the patient from sleep, the absence of fever, and the bilateral nature of the crackles extending from the bases.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The diagnostic tetrad of profound orthopnea, pink frothy sputum, bilateral fine crackles, and an S3 gallop definitively points to acute cardiogenic pulmonary oedema secondary to left ventricular failure. The severely elevated blood pressure and marked hypoxia indicate a hypertensive acute heart failure crisis. Immediate after-hours intervention was life-saving; failing to acutely offload the heart with vasodilators and diuretics before morning hours would have resulted in rapid progression to hypoxaemic respiratory arrest.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Sublingual Glyceryl Trinitrate (GTN) 400mcg stat to reduce preload and afterload. Administered IV Furosemide 40mg. Called 000 for Priority 1 transport for CPAP/BiPAP therapy. Patient kept strictly seated upright with legs dangling.
Positive Behavioral Change: By never skipping your daily fluid tablet (Furosemide), you ensure your kidneys constantly clear out the extra water, preventing it from backing up into your lungs while you sleep.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Ambulance transport for continuous positive airway pressure (CPAP).
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Handover to paramedics; cardiology follow-up required post-discharge.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance dispatched. Should the patient become drowsy or confused (signs of hypercapnia), or if breathing stops, the spouse is instructed to assist the patient to the floor and initiate CPR per 000 instructions.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Your heart is struggling to pump effectively, causing fluid to leak into your lungs. This requires immediate hospital treatment with specialized oxygen masks and intravenous medications.
Clinical Course: With prompt administration of nitrates, diuretics, and hospital-based breathing support, the fluid will clear rapidly over the next 12 to 24 hours.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not lie down under any circumstances, as the fluid will spread across your lungs and worsen your breathing. Keep your legs dangling off the bed to pool the blood in your legs and take the strain off your heart.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: I gave you a tablet under the tongue to immediately open your blood vessels and lower your blood pressure, which makes it much easier for your heart to pump. The injection will make you urinate out the excess fluid.
Actionable Steps: Try to calm your breathing by focusing on breathing out slowly. Keep your hands on your knees and stay sitting completely upright until the paramedics put you on their stretcher.

